package cajeta.net;

import cajeta.net.LoadShedPolicy;

/**
 * The **connection-limit configuration** common to both server accept
 * models (plan item **NET-4.4**) — the small bundle of resource caps the
 * [`Server`](Server.cajeta) (Model A) and
 * [`SharedPoolServer`](SharedPoolServer.cajeta) (Model B) share, so the
 * backpressure surface is identical regardless of dispatch model. It groups
 * the four knobs the spec
 * ([`docs/Net.md` §Backpressure](../../../../../docs/Net.md#backpressure))
 * names:
 *
 *   1. **`maxConnections`** — the max-concurrent-connections cap, enforced
 *      by a [`ConnectionLimiter`](ConnectionLimiter.cajeta) (a semaphore-
 *      style permit pool) gating admission. `0` ⇒ unbounded (no cap).
 *   2. **`listenBacklog`** — the kernel `listen(2)` accept-queue depth
 *      (NET-1.4's [`TcpListener.bindWithBacklog`](TcpListener.cajeta#bindWithBacklog)
 *      knob), surfaced here as config so a server's fan-in tolerance is
 *      configured in one place.
 *   3. **`readBufferCap`** / **`writeBufferCap`** — the per-connection
 *      read/write buffer caps (bytes). A slow reader/writer is bounded to
 *      this much buffered data before its own
 *      [`AsyncReader`](AsyncReader.cajeta) / [`AsyncWriter`](AsyncWriter.cajeta)
 *      fiber parks — the high-water mark of the NET-11.5 watermark
 *      backpressure, so a slow client blocks **its own** writer fiber
 *      without consuming unbounded memory or stalling other connections
 *      (the `ServerTests.slowClientDoesNotStallOthers` acceptance).
 *   4. **`shedPolicy`** — what to do with a connection that would exceed
 *      `maxConnections`: [`REFUSE`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta#REFUSE) (close it)
 *      or [`BLOCK`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta#BLOCK) (park the accept loop until
 *      a slot frees).
 *
 * ## Immutable value, sensible defaults
 *
 * An instance is a read-only snapshot built once at server-construction
 * time (the [`Server.builder()`](Server.cajeta) NET-4.5 surface threads it
 * through) — never mutated after a server starts. [`defaults`](#defaults)
 * supplies the out-of-the-box posture; the `withX` copy-helpers build a
 * tweaked variant without a builder, the same shape
 * [`HttpParserLimits`](http/HttpParserLimits.cajeta) uses.
 */
public class ConnectionLimits {
    /** Default cap on concurrent connections (`1024`). `0` ⇒ unbounded. */
    public static final int32 DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS = 1024;

    /** Default kernel `listen` backlog (`128`) — matches NET-1.4's default. */
    public static final int32 DEFAULT_LISTEN_BACKLOG = 128;

    /** Default per-connection read buffer cap (`65536` bytes — 64 KiB). */
    public static final int32 DEFAULT_READ_BUFFER_CAP = 65536;

    /** Default per-connection write buffer cap (`65536` bytes — 64 KiB). */
    public static final int32 DEFAULT_WRITE_BUFFER_CAP = 65536;

    /**
     * Max concurrent connections the server admits. A
     * [`ConnectionLimiter`](ConnectionLimiter.cajeta) is sized to this; the
     * (cap+1)th admission is shed per [`shedPolicy`](#shedPolicy). `0` ⇒ no
     * cap (the limiter is bypassed). Field index 1 (after the vtable).
     */
    public int32 maxConnections;

    /**
     * The kernel `listen(2)` backlog — how many accepted-but-not-yet-
     * `accept()`ed connections the kernel queues before it starts refusing
     * at the SYN level. Passed through to
     * [`TcpListener.bindWithBacklog`](TcpListener.cajeta#bindWithBacklog).
     */
    public int32 listenBacklog;

    /**
     * Per-connection read buffer cap (bytes): the high-water mark a
     * connection's inbound buffering is bounded to before its reader fiber
     * parks. Bounds the memory a single slow consumer can pin.
     */
    public int32 readBufferCap;

    /**
     * Per-connection write buffer cap (bytes): the high-water mark a
     * connection's outbound buffering is bounded to before its writer fiber
     * parks. A slow client (one not draining its socket) thus blocks **its
     * own** writer here rather than letting the server buffer unboundedly —
     * the property that keeps one slow client from stalling the others.
     */
    public int32 writeBufferCap;

    /**
     * The [`LoadShedPolicy`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta) ordinal applied when a
     * connection would exceed [`maxConnections`](#maxConnections):
     * [`REFUSE`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta#REFUSE) (default) or
     * [`BLOCK`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta#BLOCK).
     */
    public int32 shedPolicy;

    /**
     * Build an explicit limit set. User code usually starts from
     * [`defaults`](#defaults) and tweaks with the `withX` helpers rather
     * than calling this directly; it exists so the
     * [`Server.builder()`](Server.cajeta) (NET-4.5) can assemble a fully
     * specified instance in one shot.
     *
     * @param maxConnections concurrent-connection cap (`0` ⇒ unbounded).
     * @param listenBacklog  kernel `listen` backlog depth.
     * @param readBufferCap  per-connection read high-water (bytes).
     * @param writeBufferCap per-connection write high-water (bytes).
     * @param shedPolicy     a [`LoadShedPolicy`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta)
     *                       ordinal.
     */
    public ConnectionLimits(int32 maxConnections, int32 listenBacklog,
                            int32 readBufferCap, int32 writeBufferCap,
                            int32 shedPolicy) {
        this.maxConnections = maxConnections;
        this.listenBacklog = listenBacklog;
        this.readBufferCap = readBufferCap;
        this.writeBufferCap = writeBufferCap;
        this.shedPolicy = shedPolicy;
    }

    /**
     * The out-of-the-box limit set: `1024` concurrent connections, a `128`
     * kernel backlog, 64 KiB read + write buffer caps, and the
     * [`REFUSE`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta#REFUSE) shed policy (shed the surplus
     * rather than queue it — the latency-favouring default). The posture a
     * [`Server.bind`](Server.cajeta#bind) gets when the caller does not
     * configure limits.
     */
    public static #ConnectionLimits defaults() {
        return heap ConnectionLimits(ConnectionLimits.DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS,
                                     ConnectionLimits.DEFAULT_LISTEN_BACKLOG,
                                     ConnectionLimits.DEFAULT_READ_BUFFER_CAP,
                                     ConnectionLimits.DEFAULT_WRITE_BUFFER_CAP,
                                     LoadShedPolicy.REFUSE);
    }

    /**
     * A copy of these limits with a different concurrent-connection cap.
     * `0` ⇒ unbounded. The copy-on-write helper that keeps a
     * [`ConnectionLimits`](ConnectionLimits.cajeta) effectively immutable —
     * the [`HttpParserLimits`](http/HttpParserLimits.cajeta) `withX` shape.
     */
    public #ConnectionLimits withMaxConnections(int32 max) {
        return heap ConnectionLimits(max, this.listenBacklog,
                                     this.readBufferCap, this.writeBufferCap,
                                     this.shedPolicy);
    }

    /** A copy with a different kernel `listen` backlog. */
    public #ConnectionLimits withListenBacklog(int32 backlog) {
        return heap ConnectionLimits(this.maxConnections, backlog,
                                     this.readBufferCap, this.writeBufferCap,
                                     this.shedPolicy);
    }

    /** A copy with different per-connection read + write buffer caps. */
    public #ConnectionLimits withBufferCaps(int32 readCap, int32 writeCap) {
        return heap ConnectionLimits(this.maxConnections, this.listenBacklog,
                                     readCap, writeCap, this.shedPolicy);
    }

    /**
     * A copy with a different [`LoadShedPolicy`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta) —
     * e.g. `defaults().withShedPolicy(LoadShedPolicy.BLOCK)` to favour
     * throughput over shedding.
     */
    public #ConnectionLimits withShedPolicy(int32 policy) {
        return heap ConnectionLimits(this.maxConnections, this.listenBacklog,
                                     this.readBufferCap, this.writeBufferCap,
                                     policy);
    }

    /**
     * `true` when [`maxConnections`](#maxConnections) is `0` — the
     * "no concurrent-connection cap" sentinel a
     * [`ConnectionLimiter`](ConnectionLimiter.cajeta) reads to bypass
     * admission gating entirely (every `tryAdmit` succeeds).
     */
    public boolean isUnbounded() {
        return this.maxConnections == 0;
    }
}
